// ADMIT TWIN for fail/bc_d1r2_place_write_field_held (Door A). Same place
// write, same holder graph — only the last use of the written place moves
// BEFORE the mutation, so NLL retires the loan and `c.bump()` is legal. Without
// this half the refusal above is indistinguishable from a checker that made
// every assignment target a lexical borrow of its RHS root: a place write that
// can never be released again reds nothing and reads exactly like the fix.
// VALUES are asserted, not just the exit code: `n` must be the PRE-mutation 1
// (the borrow was read while it was live) and `c.v` the POST-mutation 2.
//
// D1 round 3 / F6 — THE THIRD ASSERTION IS NOW PRESENT. It was withheld here
// as a measured OVER-REFUSAL: `if z != 9i64 { return 3; }` after the mutation
// used to refuse with "cannot borrow 'c' as mutable", because a use of the
// loan's TARGET (`z`, the referent the place write overwrote) was counted as a
// use of its HOLDER. Mechanism: ref_borrow_sources_ was keyed per BINDING with
// no field path, so `w.b = c.mk()` could not express "the source of w.b was
// replaced"; the stale source `z` survived, place_write_loans re-rooted it onto
// the c-loan as a co-holder, and holders_last_use then maxed over `z`'s later
// read. Fixed by keying ref_borrow_sources_ by PLACE ("w", "w.b", "w.b.p") so a
// write erases the subtree it overwrites (borrow_check.cpp: place_of /
// place_under / erase_ref_sources_under / collect_ref_sources_paths).
// Controls kept at the fix: an unrelated same-typed local never triggered it;
// the plain-assign spelling (`b = c.mk()`) always admitted because SAssign
// ERASED where FieldWrite MERGED; a &-param source never triggered it. The
// Door F twin (pass/bc_d1r2_call_out_param_admits) carried this assertion all
// along, which is what localised the defect to the direct place-write spelling.
// KEEP-RED NEIGHBOURS — restoring an assertion into an admit is an INVERTED
// pin, so it is only evidence while the refusals over the SAME rule stay red.
// Verified red alongside it: fail/bc_d1r2_place_write_field_held (Door A's own
// leak), fail/bc_d1r3_f6_place_write_use_after_mut (this exact program with the
// last use of `w` AFTER the mutation) and fail/bc_d1r3_f6_place_write_source_escapes
// (the dangling-source direction, which the PLACE keys had to preserve).
package bc_d1r2_place_write_field_admits;
#[borrow_carrying]
struct B { p: &i64 }
struct C { v: i64 }
impl C {
    fn mk(&self) -> B { return B { p: &self.v }; }
    fn bump(&mut self) { self.v = self.v + 1i64; }
}
struct Wrap { b: B }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let z: i64 = 9i64;
    let mut w: Wrap = Wrap { b: B { p: &z } };
    w.b = c.mk();
    let n: i64 = *w.b.p;
    c.bump();
    if n != 1i64 { return 1; }
    if c.v != 2i64 { return 2; }
    if z != 9i64 { return 3; }
    return 0;
}
